{"parentUuid":"4879a508-24ec-45aa-966c-febd5c8ac174","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ebe28a4c-3f9c-4c73-8897-f1f47d0db79f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017xMmmf9h2haCPVzuQRyVJM","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: my-feedback\ndescription: >-\n  File a feedback or enhancement request FROM the current project INTO another\n  project's inbound feedback folder, following that project's own README\n  convention. The cross-project complement of /process-feedback (which works the\n  *current* project's inbox). Use whenever the user says \"/my-feedback to X\",\n  \"send feedback to X\", \"file a feedback item to X\", \"request an enhancement\n  from X\", \"drop a note in X's feedback folder\", or \"ask X to add/fix Y\". The\n  target project MUST be named — error if it is missing. Keywords: feedback,\n  cross-project request, enhancement request, upstream, downstream, service\n  boundary, file feedback, request a feature, inter-service.\n---\n\n# my-feedback\n\n**Role:** the *outbound* end of the agent↔agent channel — it files a request into another\nproject's intake, where their `/process-feedback` picks it up and (if codeable) runs it\nthrough their `/develop` pipeline. It is the send side; `/process-feedback` is the receive\nside. **Never** cross the service boundary to fix the other project's code yourself — the\ndeliverable is an auditable request, not a patch.\n\nWrite one actionable feedback file into **another** project's intake folder, so\nthe team that owns the boundary can act on it via their `/process-feedback`. The\ncurrent project is the **From**; the named project is the **To**.\n=== process-feedback head ===\n---\nname: process-feedback\ndescription: >-\n  Process the current project's inbound feedback folder end-to-end: first sync\n  any open GitHub issues on the project's repo into the intake as new feedback\n  files (deduped against already-tracked issues), then read every open item,\n  evaluate it against real code, and for work that belongs to this\n  project queue it as scheduled PRDs via /develop (never implement inline),\n  archive the item as processed **the moment it is queued** (the scheduler owns\n  execution from there), and fold lessons back into the folder's README so\n  future feedback (written by other agents/projects, or GitHub issue filers)\n  gets better. Use whenever the user says \"/process-feedback\",\n  \"review the feedback folder\", \"work through the feedback\", \"any open\n  feedback?\", \"check the GitHub issues\", \"triage the GitHub repo\", or drops\n  new files into session-manager-operations/feedback/.\n  Keywords: feedback, intake, cross-project requests, process feedback, triage\n  feedback, GitHub issues.\nmodel: opus\n---\n\n=== referenced elsewhere ===\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/my-feedback/SKILL.md\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/find-opportunity/SKILL.md\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/explain-to-me/SKILL.md\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/process-feedback/SKILL.md\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/optimize-kpi/SKILL.md\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/project-status/SKILL.md\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/develop/standards.md\nplugins/session-manager-dev/skills/develop/SKILL.md\nsrc/main/__tests__/scheduler-boot-orphans.test.cjs\nsrc/main/templates/PRD_AUTHORING.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b78df52b-0306-4579-a7b1-0cc42bd842c5","timestamp":"2026-08-01T06:08:01.256Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nname: my-feedback\ndescription: >-\n  File a feedback or enhancement request FROM the current project INTO another\n  project's inbound feedback folder, following that project's own README\n  convention. The cross-project complement of /process-feedback (which works the\n  *current* project's inbox). Use whenever the user says \"/my-feedback to X\",\n  \"send feedback to X\", \"file a feedback item to X\", \"request an enhancement\n  from X\", \"drop a note in X's feedback folder\", or \"ask X to add/fix Y\". The\n  target project MUST be named — error if it is missing. Keywords: feedback,\n  cross-project request, enhancement request, upstream, downstream, service\n  boundary, file feedback, request a feature, inter-service.\n---\n\n# my-feedback\n\n**Role:** the *outbound* end of the agent↔agent channel — it files a request into another\nproject's intake, where their `/process-feedback` picks it up and (if codeable) runs it\nthrough their `/develop` pipeline. It is the send side; `/process-feedback` is the receive\nside. **Never** cross the service boundary to fix the other project's code yourself — the\ndeliverable is an auditable request, not a patch.\n\nWrite one actionable feedback file into **another** project's intake folder, so\nthe team that owns the boundary can act on it via their `/process-feedback`. The\ncurrent project is the **From**; the named project is the **To**.\n=== process-feedback head ===\n---\nname: process-feedback\ndescription: >-\n  Process the current project's inbound feedback folder end-to-end: first sync\n  any open GitHub issues on the project's repo into the intake as new feedback\n  files (deduped against already-tracked issues), then read every open item,\n  evaluate it against real code, and for work that belongs to this\n  project queue it as scheduled PRDs via /develop (never implement inline),\n  archive the item as processed **the moment it is queued** (the scheduler owns\n  execution from there), and fold lessons back into the folder's README so\n  future feedback (written by other agents/projects, or GitHub issue filers)\n  gets better. Use whenever the user says \"/process-feedback\",\n  \"review the feedback folder\", \"work through the feedback\", \"any open\n  feedback?\", \"check the GitHub issues\", \"triage the GitHub repo\", or drops\n  new files into session-manager-operations/feedback/.\n  Keywords: feedback, intake, cross-project requests, process feedback, triage\n  feedback, GitHub issues.\nmodel: opus\n---