ck` skills, and the scheduler's 5-minute feedback sweep — is gone; the proposal IS the work item, already carrying its own session and PRD directory. The in-app producer is `lib/rcaFeedbackHook.cjs` (a failed PRD files a proposal; a re-trigger enriches the pending one in place rather than duplicating it).\"\"\")\n\ns = s.replace(\"\"\"Any new feature touching sessions, navigation, or per-project state must map onto this TAB→operations-root→EPIC→session hierarchy rather than inventing a parallel scoping scheme.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"- **Single-writer law over the operations root** (`src/main/lib/opsOwnership.cjs`). Every `session-manager-operations/<namespace>/` folder has exactly ONE owning surface that may write it; everyone else reads freely. Owners today: `prompt-sessions → epics`, `scheduler → scheduler`, `project-brief → project-home`, `feedback → feedback`, `browser → browser`. It is **fail-closed** — a write by an undeclared writer, or into a namespace with no declared owner, throws. Every write path enforces it (`config.cjs`'s `writeTextAtomic`/`writeBinaryAtomic`/`writeJson`/`writeJsonSync` via `opts.writer`, plus `lib/epicMint.cjs` and `lib/queueStore.cjs`, which use raw `fs`). Renderer callers declare theirs through the IPC payload (`config.writeJson(path, data, 'epics')`). Cross-writer exceptions are narrow, path-scoped **delegations** declared in one table — today only the scheduler writing `prompt-sessions/active-index.json`. Adding a namespace or writer is a deliberate edit to that file, not an implicit consequence of calling `fs.writeFile`.\n\nAny new feature touching sessions, navigation, or per-project state must map onto this TAB→operations-root→EPIC→session hierarchy rather than inventing a parallel scoping scheme.\"\"\")\n\ns = s.replace(\"\"\"All session-manager per-project operations (feedback intake, the `/explain-to-me` knowledge base) live under `session-manager-operations/` at the repo root — e.g. `session-manager-operations/feedback/`, `session-m